we actually have some good news for you  today there's so much bad news in  American politics that it's sort of  notable to have some good news and it  relates to something called the national  popular vote interstate compact okay  we've talked for a long time about  popular vote versus electoral vote for  deciding who becomes president of the  United States and as we've talked about  before one of the reasons that an  electoral vote was initially established  in the United States was to try to  prevent candidates from merely focusing  on population centers and to try to  ensure that lower population places are  also visited and lobbied so to speak by  presidential candidates things have  changed significantly with media and the  internet the way that it exists today  the physical presence of presidential  candidates in high or low population  areas has never been less important but  more importantly there is a concern now  that the electoral vote has actually run  counter to the will of the people for  example if we look at recent  presidential elections we look at the  last five in 2016 Donald Trump won  despite losing the popular vote in 2012  President Obama won both the electoral  and popular vote in 2008 President Obama  won the electoral vote and just crushed  the popular vote by about 10 million  votes in 2004 George W Bush won and won  the popular vote but in 2000 Bush won  having lost the popular vote so in two  out of the last five or two put in a  different way in 40% of the last five  presidential elections the  president-elect did not win the popular  vote now you can make the argument that  if there was a national popular vote  instead of an electoral college the  candidates would run their campaigns  differently but that's the case with any  system no matter what system you have  candidates are going to run their  campaign in the way that they think is  going to be most advantageous given the  system that there is now how could we  get rid of the electoral  one way which is really difficult is a  constitutional amendment it's  extraordinarily difficult to make that  happen but there's another possibility  which I've been telling you about which  is called the national popular vote  interstate compact it gets around the  electoral vote without making any  changes to the Constitution each state  decides how they award their electoral  votes and what states can decide is to  award their electoral votes not to the  candidate that wins their state but to  the candidate that wins the national  popular vote constitutional because  states have a lot of power in deciding  how they are going to reward their  electoral votes the problem is an issue  of math you need 270 electoral votes to  win the presidency so until you have  enough states signing on to the npv I  see such that they have 270 electoral  votes it doesn't go into effect because  there would be no point it wouldn't work  currently there are 11 states plus  Washington DC that have signed on to the  npv I see 172 electoral votes in total  that's 98 short of the number required  now these are all blue states so far and  that makes sense why would red states  join the npv I see if they correctly  perceive that it would help Democrats  and hurt Republicans it's likely that  Delaware would sign on at some point  that's only worth three electoral votes  but the big news that I have for you  today is that Colorado is now expected  to join they have nine electoral votes  and that would get us to a hundred and  eighty-one closing the gap to only 89  remaining to get to 270  the reason Colorado is so important is  because it's not a super blue state by  any means the way that most of the other  states that are part of this compact are  it's more of a swing state and the path  forward to pass national popular vote in  states that vote red is getting  Democrats elected as governors and two  state legislatures  because the path forward it probably  includes Delaware like 